arenafps (working title) credits

Everything in this game is made by its own code, but for the fighters' body and motion below:
  - textures (concrete, gravel, grass, paint, steel, wood): procedural PBR sets (albedo, normal, roughness,
    occlusion) generated at load by src/gfx.c, the same generators as cartwheel-examples' demolition derby
    game (MIT, https://github.com/monteslu/cartwheel-examples),
  - sounds: synthesised at start from sine and noise in src/fx.c,
  - models: the fighters' kit (helmet, visor, plates, pouches, pack, boots, the rifle), the four first person weapons
    with gloved hands, the six pickups and the arena props (crates, barrels, barriers, columns, planters, pipes, vents,
    floodlights, consoles) are modelled and rigged by our own Blender scripts in tools/blender/ (soldier.py, weapons.py,
    pickups.py, props.py, run with tools/blender/build.sh and tools/fetch-anims.sh, headless Blender 5) and exported as
    glTF into models/. Blender itself is only the authoring tool (GPL); the exported models are our own work under this
    game's licence,
  - level geometry: tools/make-levels.mjs lays the rooms, walls, roofs and doors out as CSG shapes in levels/*.cwscene and
    the engine's cwcsg tool bakes them into levels/*.cwcsg; stairs, trim and the outer shell are boxes built in code,
  - colour grades: tools/make-luts.mjs writes the three .cube LUTs in luts/,
  - decal sprites (bullet hole, scorch): tools/make-decals.mjs writes textures/*.png,
  - explosion flipbooks and scorch marks (textures/fx/*.png): generated by the cartwheel-engine's
    tools/fx/bake-flipbooks.mjs (procedural, CC0, listed in the engine NOTICE),
  - HUD and menus: the engine's shell and its built-in UI font.

Fighters' body and motion: from CC0 libraries
  sources     Quaternius, "Universal Animation Library" (Standard, free) and "Universal Animation Library 2" (Standard,
              free), CC0 1.0 Universal (public domain dedication):
                https://quaternius.com/packs/universalanimationlibrary.html
                https://opengameart.org/content/universal-animation-library
                https://opengameart.org/content/universal-animation-library-2
                https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/
              The libraries' licence notes are in tools/blender/cc0/ual (LICENSE-ual1.txt, LICENSE-ual2.txt); the files
              are fetched and checked by tools/fetch-anims.sh. CC0 asks for nothing; the credit is given anyway.
  body        the first library's mannequin and its 65 bone skeleton (decimated), worn under the kit as the fabric suit
              (tools/blender/soldier.py)
  motion      tools/blender/anims.py (run headless in Blender) picks the clips the game plays (the stand, the walk, the
              sprint, the jump, the landing, the chest and head hits, the overhand throw, the fall), cuts and joins
              them, and makes the clips the libraries lack from their motion: the walk and the sprint turned at the
              hips 45 and 90 degrees with the chest kept forward and run backwards (eight directions), the throw
              mirrored onto the left arm, the chest hit turned to come from behind and either side; the arms on the
              rifle (the hold, the recoil, the reload, the rifle jab, the rifle lowered for a throw) are solved there on
              the rig. The result is anim/ual.glb (with the mannequin to carry the rig); the engine's packer retargets
              it onto each soldier (models/soldier_*.glb.cwimport).

Fonts: the engine's built-in UI font; its licence is in the cartwheel-engine NOTICE (open font licence).

Third-party assets: the CC0 body and motion sources above.

Engine libraries: see the cartwheel-engine NOTICE (three.c, Box3D, Recast and Detour (zlib), RVO2 (Apache-2.0),
miniaudio and the rest).

